00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.149 align:start position:0%
 
joining us now is Helen King a former  assistant commissioner at the  Metropolitan Police Service thank you so  much for coming to the studio this  morning it's appreciate it good morning  um I just want to talk about a story  that broke yesterday uh the head of the  police Watchdog resigning because the  police are investigating a historical  allegation about him  I mean you really couldn't make it up  well obviously I don't know any more  than is in the Press already and clearly  what needs to happen is that  investigation needs to go ahead no doubt  the individual concerned is under under  huge pressure and his family and so on  as well and that process has to run but  at the same time everyone the public  complainants police officers need to be  able to have confidence in the iopc as  well and the the the investigations that  they carry out I don't think you're  completely right of course that we don't  know the details of the allegation it  would be absolutely wrong to make any uh  judgments there  um but I guess the police investigation  it seems has been going on for some time  and yet Michael look would only stepped  aside when the Home Secretary told him  to either quit or be suspended it feels  like there's question marks that the  iopc was even aware of the allegation I  as I say I know no more than is in the  Press it's important that the police  complaint system is one that both  officers and the public can have  confidence in and I think one of the the  constructive debate that is happening at  the moment is looking at the way  allegations against the police including  internal police misconduct  investigations are dealt with to make  sure that police Chiefs can sack police  officers where that's appropriate and  that those investigations can be  concluded more quickly and in ways that  have public and officer confidence yeah  to talk about the public confidence in  the police but also other institutions  as well because it does feel like  there's been I guess already almost  classed as like a misogyny crisis and  some of these institutions and the  London Farm Brigade are described as  institutionally misogynistic and racist  reports that people have in their helmet  filled with urine Muslim offices having  pork sausages put in their pockets and  of course the Met police too the some of  the WhatsApp messages exchange between  officers joking about rape and domestic  violence do you think there is  institutional misogyny and racism in  some of these organizations  um let's be frank the the behaviors  you've described are absolutely  despicable  um and something that both we the public  but also the good the many thousands of  of really good committed officers  firefighters and so on will also find  disgusting and what dealt with  but let's be frank this isn't new I  think in a sense it's good that these  conversations are being had it's  following on from the me too movement  um let's look at Parliament you know  you've been a political correspondent I  suspect that you know there's been  um maybe old boys clubs and and networks  in a wide range of Institutions I think  certainly Parliament is not somewhere to  throw stones I think you're probably  definitely right exactly public and  private sector but absolutely right that  it's now being exposed and of course  those who are put in positions of trust  which police officers firefighters  emergency workers are they do an  incredible job but we need to be able to  have confidence in them so it's right  that these behaviors are exposed and  dealt with but at the same time we need  to think about how do we attract and  keep really good stuff those who have  the moral Courage the ethical resilience  to do these really difficult jobs where  they're dealing with tragedy they're  dealing with horrible situations they're  dealing often with hostility from the  public they feel they're under attack by  the media by politicians and now we look  at the the state of our public services  and the amount of work they're having to  do not always with the resources that  they need  so I think there's a question for all of  us yes for leaders of these institutions  but also for us to say how do we make  sure that we have the public services  that we deserve and we support those who  are doing these incredibly difficult  jobs on our behalf I'm interested on  your thoughts on what can change what  can actually practically be done and the  report in November example for example  found some recent examples of people who  were cleared to join the police when  they had past convictions for indecent  exposure assaults on women even an  arrest for rape do you think the vetting  is up to scratch  um vetting can always be improved but I  do think it's a blunt tool you can  generally only rely upon convictions  and of course people with the wrong  attitudes who are joining for the wrong  reasons  um may be hiding that so you've got to  have the right training you've got to  have the right type of supervision and  Leadership and a culture where people  will be reported by their peers and then  a system that can push them out of the  organization at the moment police  regulations are unwieldy police Chiefs  aren't the ones who are making the  decisions about who should be sacked or  not  um and I think it's right that that is  being opened up question yeah that was  interesting because the new met police  chief samark Rowley um in a recent  interview said that you know he has to  employ a hundred people who are only  allowed to work in back room roles and  he said it's completely mad that I had  to employ people like that as police  officers who you can't trust have  contact with the public so is there a  problem about effective not be able to  stack people yes  um some years ago and I understand why  from a public perception point of view  um the makeup of police misconduct  panels was changed so that there's a  legally qualified chair rather than a  senior police officer who chairs those  panels I chaired dozens of those panels  during my my service and my experience  of sitting with lay members is that they  tend to be more sympathetic towards  police officers  um maybe more understanding uh you know  in a way that I felt actually they  shouldn't be and police officers have to  maintain the highest standards of  behavior because they're enforcing the  law on fellowship with fellow citizens  um so I do think that's one of a number  of things that should be looked at in  terms of the way police misconduct is  dealt with you know you obviously in the  worked for the police did you witness  any of this kind of behavior or was it  things that are I guess more generally  kept within groups of men or did you see  anything or right by the by the time I  joined the met I was very senior I was a  police officer for 30 years in in three  different police forces  um I'm not sure it's very helpful to  start talking about incidents that  happened 35 years ago but I think all  women who've worked in male dominated  organizations  um  um people from LGBT communities people  from different ethnic backgrounds when  you're in a minority within an  organization and that can feel like  quite a lonely place I think the recent  report about the fire service is is  worth a read and it includes in that an  example of what a good team looks like  where everyone feels that they're  pulling together and the different  strengths are seen to be making the team  more stronger  um themselves but I think sometimes when  you're the only one and the team ethos  is about  um a very traditional view  yeah which which emphasizes maybe the  physicality of policing or firefighting  and not recognizing that actually the  breadth of skills that you need to work  effectively as a team means that you  need different people within that team  yeah I thought one of the really  interesting parts of that report into  the London fire brigade was the figures  on just how white a male an organization  it is  um you've said before that policing is  an attractive career for sexual  predators bullies and bigots now I know  that you've also emphasized the really  good people who work in the police and  it's important to say that but what did  you mean by that well  as I as I said before as a police  officer you have power over your fellow  citizens and also the way  um policing tends to be portrayed in the  media it's about Pub fights it's about  driving cars fast it's the more Macho  side if you like of the role whereas  actually the figures show now about 80  percent of police incidents don't even  involve a crime uh the police more and  more are picking up issues that come  from people in Mental Health crisis  people with all sorts of vulnerabilities  missing children missing elderly people  and so on so  um  the way policing is seen if you start up  from from a position where maybe you are  a bully or a bigot it looks like an  attractive career to you and so that's  why recruitment training  um and an internal culture that does  report those who really don't deserve to  carry the warrant card or to wear the  uniform and there is increasing evidence  of that  um and Reporting hotlines and so on that  that people can use I went on one of  those websites the other day where  people give Anonymous reviews of their  employers and when you look at the one  for the Metropolitan Police there are a  number of people who say they're they're  former officers complaining that you  can't trust your colleagues any net  anymore because they might report on you  yeah and I actually thought that that  was a very positive sign that people are  realizing you can't get away with  behavior where maybe you thought you  were safe in the past just to pick up  very quickly before we end on something  you just said 80 of police incidents  don't involve a crime yes I'm quite  staggered by that do you think that's  because you're effectively having to  take on a greater role in Pub where  Public Services other public services  like the Health Service or whatever are  stretched it's always been true but I  suspect even more so now  um policing sometimes gets called The  Social Services of Last Resort  of all the organizations that are  available 24 hours a day seven days a  week the police in effect the last one's  left standing and I think we would all  want somebody to go and help the most  vulnerable in their hour of need but the  point that Mark Rowley is is making at  the moment is that that does become a  distraction and depletes resources from  those incidents that really only the  police can deal with and that's going to  be a challenge for Society for those  um deciding where where taxpayers money  goes within the public sector  fascinating didn't realize that at all  thank you so much for being on the  program today really interesting to talk

